Subject: WebView: stop the WebView zygote when no renderer has needed it for a while

0005 starts the WebView zygote with the first WebView renderer rather than at boot, but from
then on the zygote ran until the guest stopped. A DSA guest idle for two hours still held it
(PSS 11 MiB) long after the app that showed web content had closed.

The activity manager now checks the zygote every minute once a renderer has started from it
(ProcessList, on the process start handler with its lock held). When no process started from
the WebView zygote has existed, or been starting, for three minutes, it stops the zygote
(WebViewZygote.stopIdleZygote) and stops checking. WebViewZygote.getProcess, which starts each
renderer, starts the zygote again when there is none, as for the first renderer, and the checks
begin again. A renderer is started either on the process start handler or with the activity
manager's lock held, and its process record exists before it starts, so a start never finds the
zygote stopping under it. The WebView package is kept; a change of WebView provider still stops
the zygote as before. Two log lines tell the cost:

  ProcessList: Stopped the WebView zygote: no WebView renderer for N s; the next WebView starts
  it again
  WebViewZygote: Started the WebView zygote in N ms

Three minutes keep the zygote for an app that opens one web page after another. The first
WebView after a stop waits for the zygote to start again (a few hundred milliseconds).

Applies to frameworks/base at 94b4c163b7dfe5ce3607f7bb8456f9573f7de57d (Android 17), after 0015.
